{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793372910,"nonce":"WXd1bB6XSHxne3su6m+rUR8rXXw=","message":"What Blinc's Sketch teaches (Blinc docs canvas-kit/sketches.md, players.md, interactive.md; crates blinc_media and blinc_gpu; ashui.draw.Sketch):\n1. A framework app is a live Player: Blinc's `Player` trait is the contract for external time-based sources (draw_at(ctx, rect, t), seek, set_playing, duration None for live content). A streamed app is a live player; pause and hidden tab are set_playing(false).\n2. Latest frame wins with a generation counter: blinc_media's VideoPlayer holds the current frame as a cheap shared object plus a counter that increments per decoded frame, so consumers skip redundant GPU uploads. Same rule as this protocol's bounded latest-frame slot.\n3. Partial uploads: blinc_gpu patches changed ranges (write_primitives_partial) because a full upload costs far more than the changed part: the argument for damage rectangles.\n4. Replay maps onto Painter: Sketch's immediate-mode Painter (fill, stroke, rect, circle, line, a transform stack, with DrawContext for gradients, clips, images and text) is close to OpenFL's Graphics commands: the natural target for the display-list replay tier (ed5ce47).\n5. Input is CanvasKit's job (hit regions, pan, zoom), not Sketch's; ashui.draw.Sketch has no input. For a game surface, raw input belongs on a focusable wrapper.\nGap: Sketch draws through DrawContext.image, which takes an existing GPU image slot; replacing pixels per frame needs ashui's `\u003csurface\u003e` (ashui 02f829d5, unscheduled) or Blinc's write_texture path exposed through blinc_abi (not found exported). Requirements sent to ashui and recorded there as 6a1fd8c5 (an ordered input event queue plus polled helpers, surfaces as first-class sketch citizens, idleness and pacing); not scheduled, darmie has not chosen a direction for sketches.","files":null}]}